Polymyalgia Rheumatica
Rheumatology & Immunology
Proximal aching/stiffness, Normal muscle strength, Rapid steroid response

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RHI-033
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe, new-onset bilateral pain and profound stiffness in the shoulders and hips, rendering an elderly patient completely unable to rise from bed, dress themselves, or comb their hair.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage revealed an elderly patient experiencing sudden, severe functional immobilization due to profound proximal stiffness, unable to perform any basic activities of daily living. An urgent after-hours physical assessment was strictly required to differentiate this from an acute proximal myopathy, severe cervical cord compression, or occult infection, and to prescribe immediate corticosteroid therapy to safely restore mobility, as deferring care until the next in-hours period left the patient immobilized, distressed, and at high risk of complications from prolonged bed rest.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I woke up yesterday and my shoulders and hips feel like they are set in concrete; I can't get out of bed, and trying to lift my arms is agonizing."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a 4-day history of severe, sudden-onset bilateral pain and deep aching in the shoulders, neck, and pelvic girdle. The most debilitating symptom is profound morning stiffness lasting longer than 2 hours. The patient describes a complete inability to rise from a chair or comb their hair due to severe pain and stiffness rather than true muscular weakness. They also note a distinct "gel phenomenon," where the stiffness returns rapidly after prolonged periods of rest during the day. Reports a low-grade fever, marked fatigue, and mild anorexia over the past week. Denies any sudden loss of vision, jaw claudication, or temporal headache (screening for concurrent Giant Cell Arteritis).
Functional Status: Currently bedbound; requires full physical assistance from spouse to transition to a seated position.
Social/Safety Concerns: Complete loss of independence for activities of daily living; spouse is elderly and struggling with physical transfers.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/82 mmHg | HR: 88 bpm | Temp: 37.4 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Patient appears highly uncomfortable, moving very slowly and rigidly, avoiding any active movement of the shoulder or hip girdles.
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal: Active range of motion in bilateral shoulders (abduction, flexion) is severely limited to less than 45 degrees due to intense pain and stiffness. Hip flexion and abduction are similarly restricted by pain. However, there is an absence of significant joint swelling, erythema, or destructive arthritis in the peripheral joints. Neurological: Once positioned, formal muscle strength testing (deltoids, hip flexors) reveals 5/5 normal muscle strength globally; the limitation is entirely pain-driven, not a true myopathic weakness. Cranial nerves intact. Temporal arteries are non-tender and pulsatile. The diagnostic tetrad is clinically obvious: severe proximal aching/stiffness, definitively normal muscle strength on examination, predicting a significantly elevated erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR) and a rapid, dramatic clinical response to low-dose corticosteroids within 48 hours.
Environmental Observations: Patient stuck in a low bed; advised on techniques for rolling to the side to rise.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking maximum doses of paracetamol and over-the-counter ibuprofen with absolutely no improvement in stiffness.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Polymyalgia Rheumatica - Status: Exacerbated (Acute immobilizing flare)
Differentials:

Polymyositis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The physical examination confirming 5/5 normal proximal muscle power (the limitation is pain, not true weakness), whereas polymyositis presents with profound, painless objective weakness.

Rheumatoid Arthritis (Elderly onset) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The strict localization to the proximal shoulder/hip girdles without small joint synovitis (wrists/hands are normal), though RA can occasionally present similarly in older adults.

Giant Cell Arteritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of temporal headache, jaw claudication, or visual disturbances, though the patient remains at high risk and requires strict safety-netting.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Functional immobilization and caregiver strain)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an archetypal, severe onset of Polymyalgia Rheumatica, resulting in acute, distressing functional immobilization. The critical diagnostic step during the bedside assessment was confirming normal proximal motor power, ruling out a primary myopathy or neurological lesion. Leaving the patient immobilized in bed until the next standard consultation period carried an unacceptable risk of deep vein thrombosis, pressure injuries, and severe caregiver burnout. The immediate prescription of low-dose corticosteroids is the definitive, safe, and necessary intervention, fully justifying the MBS 585-600 criteria for urgent after-hours care.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Initiate an immediate trial of low-dose oral corticosteroids (e.g., Prednisolone 15mg daily). Provide a referral for urgent baseline blood work (ESR, CRP, Full Blood Count) to be completed via the regular GP within 48 hours. The dramatic response to this specific steroid dose serves as both treatment and a diagnostic confirmation tool.
Positive Behavioral Change: Medication timing: Take the Prednisolone exactly at 7:00 AM every morning with breakfast; executing this precise timing directly targets the peak of the inflammatory morning stiffness, and the rapid, profound relief of pain will definitively reinforce daily adherence. Paced mobilization: Sit on the edge of the bed for a full 2 minutes before standing; performing this specific delay allows the profound 'gel' stiffness to ease slightly, actively preventing a sudden, painful mechanical fall upon waking.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Spouse advised to assist by providing a firm chair rather than a low, soft sofa to ease rising.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 days to assess the expected dramatic clinical response to the steroid trial and review blood results.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department immediately if you develop a new, severe headache on the side of your head, if your jaw hurts rapidly when chewing food, or if you experience any sudden blurriness or loss of vision in either eye (signs of Giant Cell Arteritis).

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The severe stiffness in your shoulders and hips is highly characteristic of an inflammatory condition of the muscles and joints. The prescribed steroid medication typically provides rapid, almost miraculous relief within 24 to 48 hours.
Clinical Course: The condition usually responds beautifully to low doses of steroids. However, you will likely require this medication for 1 to 2 years, slowly tapering the dose, to prevent the stiffness from returning.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

You must be vigilant for symptoms involving your head. If you develop a sudden, severe headache, jaw pain when eating, or any changes in your vision, you must go to the emergency department immediately, as this condition can sometimes involve the blood vessels in your head.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your immune system is causing severe inflammation in the lining of your large joints (shoulders and hips), causing them to seize up overnight. Standard painkillers cannot touch this inflammation. The low-dose steroid acts as a powerful, specific switch to turn off this inflammation, restoring your mobility rapidly.
Actionable Steps: Take the Prednisolone strictly every morning with breakfast. Do not attempt to force your shoulders past the pain tonight. See your regular doctor in 3 days to check your blood tests.

